A||N||17||gkozlov||Towing||||1||
Z||000000||gkozlov||11-27-2000||08:30 PM||||In December my family and I are moving to Ft. Campbell, KY. We plan on driving the Explorer and towing the Fiero behind us. The bumper says it has a 3500 lbs tow load or something to that effect. If I put a ball on it will it be safe to tow the Fiero on a dolly? I dont want to bend or warp the bumper on the Explorer.<P>If I can, should I drive up on the dolly or back up onto it? I'm not sure which would be safer.<P>Anybody?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||11-27-2000||08:59 PM||||Well the rule of thumb is 10% of the trailer weight for tounge weigth.  Figure most Fiero's are about 2800-2900 pounds you could have a trailer/dolly of about 600 pounds.<P><BR>Mark Johnson ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-27-2000||09:20 PM||||read the bumper more carefully. It should tell you tongue weight and draw weight.<P>tongue weight is the vertical load on the ball. A tow dolly shouldn't be a problem. They don't present much load that way.<P>draw weight, the bigger of the 2 numbers, is total combined weight of the towed item. In this case the Feiro plus the Dolly. This is the weight the bumper can pull, and just as importantly stop. The Fiero and the dolly are getting into the higher end of the number you posted above.<P>On a long tow you want to tow backwards. No drive axles and tranny to mess up.<P>You need to get a set of auxilary lights. these strap on the car and extend the trucks lights. You can be ticketed in most areas if you tow without these, especially if you tow backwards. You should have the headlights on low beam at all times you are on the road. A lit vehicle is less likely to get hit.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||gkozlov||11-27-2000||09:20 PM||||Are you saying that if the trailer weighs no more than 600 lbs, I'll be OK?<P>How about the Fiero? Which way should it sit on the trailer?<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||gkozlov||11-27-2000||09:25 PM||||Thanks theogre. I'll check the bumper in the morning. It's a bit dark now. <P>By the way, it's a 86 SE 4SPD. <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||11-27-2000||09:51 PM||||A class III hitch for your Explorer is about $140. With that you won't have to worry about anything.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Necromancer||11-27-2000||10:01 PM||||For what its worth, my pathetic '94 ranger (4.0 liter V6, 161k miles) has towed 3 fieros and an '86 camaro in the last year, all the trips were 300 miles or so, one way.  No problems, other than a little overheating in New Jersey on a 90+ degree day.<P>I towed 'em all on a dolly, with the front end on the dolly (they were all standard trannys) and the four ways on.<P>As always, your mileage may vary!<P>-necromancer-<BR>'85 GT ||||reg||1
Z||000007||gkozlov||11-27-2000||10:13 PM||||necromancer, <P>We have the same engines, so power is not a concern. I have the "tow package". My concern is my bumper. Did you tow yours by your bumper or did you have a hitch?||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-27-2000||10:59 PM||||yeah, I always think automatic because that's what I have. A stick doesn't care as much which way you pull.<P>I'd stick a set of lights on it either way. The way people drive anymore you need all the help you can get.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2xsess||11-28-2000||01:56 PM||||I'd highly recomend getting the classIII hitch, like was already stated you wouldn't have to worry about messing anything up.<P><BR>P.S. doesn't the Army move one vehicle for you? or are you doing a dity move?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Necromancer||11-28-2000||08:34 PM||||gkozlov:<P>Just a 2" ball bolted in the middle of the back bumper.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||gkozlov||11-28-2000||08:58 PM||||2xses'<P>I chose not to do the ditty move. Too much work for such little money.<P>The army will pay to move one vehicle by paying mileage for me driving. There are exceptions. This is not one of them. <P>I will see if I can scrape up enough money for a hitch.  If not I’ll chance it without.<P>Also check out my homepage. I’ve put a countdown until ETS on it. The link is by my signature. <P>Necromancer, <P>That’s what I’ve got. Maybe I’ll pit a ball on it and see what happens.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||007DOUG||11-29-2000||01:18 AM||||Bumpers are made to pull aluminum boats, and log spliters.<P>Hitches are made to pull cars and campers.||||reg||13
Z||000013||2xsess||11-29-2000||11:37 AM||||Think of it this way, if you have to slam on the brakes to avoid a KFC (you should know what that is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) It will probably mess up your bumper, I'll bet that you can go to either Wal-mart or U-Haul and get a hitch cheaper than you can a new bumper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>hhmmm count down to ETS... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I got 17 months<P>Jeff||||reg||1
